  "response": "The resolution of the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene controversy came from geochemistry, mineralogy and geophysics in turn, each line of work narrowing the field of plausible killers. Luis Alvarez and colleagues reported an anomalous enrichment of iridium at the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary in 1980. The Deccan Traps are flood basalts covering a large area of western India, and they remain among the largest such provinces on the planet. In the decade following that 1980 iridium report, and in sharp contrast to a lava province confined to one corner of a single subcontinent, field geologists traced boundary minerals across continents and ocean basins. Shocked quartz grains occur in Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary deposits at sites worldwide. Tektite glass spherules occur in boundary beds in Haiti and northeastern Mexico, where they form distinct basal layers.\n\nThe provenance of those grains became the crux of the dispute, since high-pressure minerals are not readily made by the quiet effusion of lava. Because the western Indian flood basalts were emplaced through deep crustal fissures, those Deccan fissure eruptions produced the shocked quartz found at the boundary. The search for a source crater proceeded by an altogether different route, through the archives of the oil industry. Glen Penfield's identification of a buried circular gravity and magnetic anomaly at Chicxulub during petroleum surveys shows that the crater lies buried beneath the Yucat\u00e1n Peninsula and the adjacent Gulf of Mexico. Its dimensions, however, admit only one of two answers: either the structure is more than 100 kilometres in diameter or it is less than 100 kilometres in diameter, one or the other and not both. Its concealment beneath the peninsula and the adjoining floor of the Gulf of Mexico indicates that the impactor itself was about 1 kilometre in diameter.\n\nOnly after that buried anomaly had been mapped in the petroleum surveys did laboratory geochronology place the competing events in time. Argon-argon dating of Chicxulub impact glass yields an age of about 66.0 million years. Deccan Traps eruptions began several hundred thousand years before the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary. Because the impacting body was about a kilometre across, the Chicxulub impact injected sulfate aerosols into the stratosphere, and Deccan Traps volcanism released large volumes of sulfur dioxide into the atmosphere; at least one of those two sulfur loadings, and perhaps both, produced the darkness and cooling registered by marine microfossils. It is the convergence of mineralogical, geophysical and radiometric results upon a single globally traceable layer, rather than any one observation, that carried the argument for most workers in the field.",
